# Repository map

Caleb's persistent memory: one git repo, synced across his five machines.
Fresh Claude Code sessions (Fable) and Mara, the OpenClaw agent, read it first. It is their only memory.
Caleb is moving from Agronomy Lab Supervisor to AI and Data Science Manager at Rock River Lab (RRL).

## Read at the start of every session

1. [SOUL.md](SOUL.md): values shared by all of Caleb's agents.
2. [FABLE.md](FABLE.md): what is specific to Fable.
3. [context/caleb-working-notes.md](context/caleb-working-notes.md): who Caleb is and how to work with him.
4. [context/feedback/DIGEST.md](context/feedback/DIGEST.md): one line per working agreement. Open a full feedback file only when its trigger fires.
5. The routed project's `MEMORY.md` ([routing](#route-to-projects-by-theme)): read the hand-written section above the generated marker yourself, and have an Opus scout return the entries that bear on the session's goals from the generated list. Don't read the whole index into your own context; the largest ones run past 70 KB.
6. That project's current handoff. Handoffs live with their code (see Conventions): the project's `project_*.md` note names the handoff's date, commit, and path in the code repo.

Items 1 to 4 arrive at launch through [CLAUDE.global.md](CLAUDE.global.md), the global Claude Code instructions, which each machine's `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` imports with one line (`agent-cooperation/agent-setup/sync-machine.ps1` writes it). If `/context` doesn't list them, read them with one Read call per file; a combined `cat` is cut off above the Bash output cap.

[MEMORY.md](MEMORY.md) and [context/TOPICS-INDEX.md](context/TOPICS-INDEX.md) are lookup indexes for when routing fails or you need reference material, not start-of-session reads.

## Repository structure

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `SOUL.md`, `FABLE.md` | Agent identity |
| `CLAUDE.global.md` | The global Claude Code instructions; each machine's `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` is a one-line import of it |
| `AGENTS.global.md` | The canonical global Codex instructions; the installer renders it as `AGENTS.md` in each machine's Codex home |
| `MEMORY.md` | Cross-project lookup index |
| `HANDOFF.md` | The newest session handoff |
| `context/feedback/` | Working agreements, summarized in `DIGEST.md` |
| `context/reference/` | Cross-project technical reference |
| `agent-cooperation/` | Skills, hooks, and delegation tools |
| `projects/<slug>/` | One folder per project |

`agent-cooperation/` has one folder per skill or tool:

- Writing: `clear-writing` (house prose style), `deliverable-polish` (final pass before shipping), `build-rrl-customer-profile-decks` (branded customer-profile decks), `adv-ag-modus-fix` (checked data-file fixes, one customer).
- Delegation: `efficient-fable` (subagents take Fable's heavy work), `efficient-astra` (the same for Codex), `codex-delegation` (Codex implements, Fable orchestrates), `codex-mcp` (local Codex server), `fireworks-delegation` (outside models review and test), `plan-review` (three-model spec council), `shared` (contracts both delegation skills use).
- Verification: `calibration-specimens` (benchmark reviewers are scored against), `specimen-capture` (stub candidates before the fix), `routing-log` (which models earn their keep), `statistical-thinking` (router plus seven statistics skills).
- Data work: `db-migration` (router plus six skills for account and master-data moves between live lab databases: source map, destination map, linkages, question register, load sheets, verify), `data-pipe` (router plus four skills for BI data pipes: spec, build, verify, publish). Built 2026-10-12/13 from the A&L, Indiana, Iowa migrations and the bi/31, bi/32 pipes; the build record, extractions, judge runs, and reviews are in `skill-builds/2026-10-12-migration-and-pipes/`.
- Memory and setup: `memory-librarian` (index builder, checker, upkeep), `hooks` (session status line and handoffs), `project-atlas` (unit-by-unit project state, opt-in), `agent-setup` (one command per machine: skills, settings keys, the CLAUDE.md pointer).

## Route to projects by theme

Folders live under `projects/`. Open a folder's `MEMORY.md` first.

