# Caleb-style code comments

Paths are relative to `<persistent-memory>/agent-cooperation/caleb-style-code-comments/`.

## Why this exists

Caleb's own comments, in code he typed himself, start lower-case in 212 of
259 sampled lines across four sources (29 of 31 in the oldest, from 2022),
run a median of seven words, end without a period, and join clauses with a
spaced hyphen. Comments agents write run 25 to 40 words, open with a
capital, and bury the one fact that matters in a paragraph. He asked for the
terse form on 2026-08-11 and twice more since. Low-noise comments keep the
gotcha findable, which is the only reason a comment is there. The evidence,
with quotes and locations, is in `references/evidence.md`.

This skill is the spec. `context/feedback/code-comment-style.md` keeps the
provenance and points here. It wins over every general writing guide,
including `clear-writing`, for anything inside a comment or docstring.

## The rules

1. **lower-case prose.** The first letter of a comment, of a sentence inside
   it, and of a docstring is lower-case. Code identifiers (`SpaceBefore`,
   `pltN`, `ReportRenderer`), quoted literals (`"No appropriate font found"`),
   and ids that appear in code or a spec (`G14`, `R1`, `Q3`) keep their
   casing, because lower-casing them would make the comment wrong.
2. **terse.** The fewest words that still carry the information. An inline
   comment under about 12 words; a block under three lines; a docstring body
   under the same budget as a block, because moving words from a comment
   into a docstring is not cutting them. Cut throat-clearing ("note that",
   "this is deliberate because"), narration of history ("until then the
   extract applied no filter"), and anything the next reader can see in the
   code.
3. **the gotcha survives, and so does its meaning.** Terseness is a
   rendering rule, not a content rule. Dates, who ruled (`caleb's call,
   2026-07-13`), the trap, the number, the reason a guard exists, and the
   cross-reference to a query or spec all stay. Compression never changes a
   claim's modality ("can reach" is not "reaches") or turns a thing that
   happened into a hypothetical ("as this once did" is not "would"). A
   comment the code contradicts is a defect: report it with file and line,
   and correct the comment only when the code is certain. Test after a
   rewrite: could the next reader still avoid the trap from the comment
   alone, and would they believe the same things?
4. **simplest explanation.** Of two phrasings that carry the same
   information, use the plainer one. No markdown emphasis, no "deliberately",
   no "literally".
5. **say what the code can't show.** The why, the trap, the external fact,
   the expected number. A comment that restates a readable line goes. A short
   step marker in a long script (`## get elems`, `# read everything first;
   nothing below writes until write_files`) is allowed, two to seven words,
   because Caleb writes those himself. When a Python block needs a step
   marker, a function with that name is usually better; that is the
   `pythonic-code` skill's call.
6. **sanity numbers stay.** Where code filters, joins, or aggregates data,
   a row count or expected result as a comment is Caleb's habit (`# down from
   1460 rows â†’ 654 âœ“`). Keep them, add them where a number was checked, and
   date one that came from a live pull.
7. **analogies from social dynamics, rarely.** When a mechanism needs an
   analogy, draw it from trust, honesty, or reporting: "don't trust the cached
   timestamp - re-check the file", "the parse run cannot be trusted as handed
   over". Caleb's own comments contain none, so one per file is already a lot.

## Rulings this skill makes

The feedback file left these open. Each is Caleb's to overturn; they are
numbered so he can.

- **C1 names.** Names keep their owners' casing: people, organizations,
  products, places, and unit-like acronyms (`Caleb`, `A&L`, `SharePoint`,
  `Watertown`, `DataDudes`, `ODBC`, `UTC`, `NIR`), and any value that appears
  in the data or as a key (`A&L London` as a location label, `Sat` as a
  calendar key). File formats and generic acronyms used as ordinary words go
  lower-case (`csv`, `json`, `sql`, `pdf`, `xml`), as do months and weekdays
  in prose. Evidence: his own NIR script writes `NIR` upper-case 11 times to
  1 and capitalises `Positron` and `GDrive`; the first version of this
  ruling lower-cased everything, and on 2026-10-15 two blind judges and
  three builders flagged the results (`a&l`, `utc`, `odbc 17`, `watertown`
  as a data value) as making the comments read wrong. The accepted rewrite
  of 2026-08-11 lower-cased `pdfsharp` and `caleb's call`; that is allowed,
  not required.
