# In / Out Button app (core)

Lightweight Windows C# applet (the "In / Out Button") that launches on startup, tracks git repos discovered under user-selected folders, runs `git pull` on sign in, and stages/commits/pushes all repos on sign out. Started 2026-05-24.

**Why:** It's the mechanism that keeps the user's git repos in sync as he moves between his three Windows machines (work desktop / home desktop / old work laptop) â€” sign in to pull everything down, sign out to push everything up. This is the same cross-machine workflow whose divergence causes the periodic merge conflicts in this very memory repo.

**How to apply:** When working on the In / Out Button app, treat the notes below as the baseline behavior. The code lives at `C:\Users\caleb\Tools\in_out_button` (see its `CLAUDE.md` for the engineering detail). Feature work layers on top of this core â€” e.g. the OneDrive-backed dataset sync in [[project-rclone-sync]].

Implementation notes:
- Built as a .NET 8 WinForms app in `C:\Users\caleb\Tools\in_out_button`.
- Search roots persist in `%APPDATA%\InOutButton\settings.json`.
- Startup launch is managed with the current user's Windows `Run` registry key.
- Git command timeout is currently 120 seconds per command.
- Sign-out commit message format is `MM-dd-yy updates`, matching the requested `MM-DD-YY updates` text pattern.
- Updated after first real sign-in failure: app now logs full git command output, not only the final line.
- Added selected-repo recovery actions: pull selected, commit local changes then `git pull --rebase` and push, or discard tracked local changes with `git reset --hard HEAD` then pull. The discard action intentionally leaves untracked files alone.
- Sign-out now rescans search roots before running git commands so repos created between sign-in and sign-out are included automatically.
- Successful git commands that emit `warning:` are now displayed as `OK with warnings`; full warning text remains visible in the log.
- 2026-08-24: batch actions separated â€” "Git pull (all)" is git-only; "Pull data (all)" / "Push data (all)" are rclone-only. Sign in/out remain combined. Batch rclone is gated to *active* repos (last commit within `RcloneActiveDays`, default 14, OR dirty working tree â€” the dirty check matters at sign-out, before the day's commit exists). Per-repo data buttons ignore the gate. Grid gained last-commit age, `â—` dirty marker, and Data `sync`/`idle` columns; full flat-modern UI restyle (cards, accent buttons, styled grid, dark log). Fixed `RcloneRunner.ProbeAsync` picking up the command-label line instead of the real version.
- 2026-09-02: three unrelated Sign-out "Failed" causes fixed in one pass (work desktop). (1) `.rclone-sync.json` entries may be single *files* (`scratch_pad/proficiency.db`); `rclone copy` fails "is a file not a directory" â€” now `copyto` for files, detected from the local type or `rclone lsjson --stat` on first pull. (2) Missing sides (entry absent locally on push after a fresh clone, e.g. `qc_modeling/data`; entry never pushed on pull â†’ rclone exit 3) are skips with a message, not failures; stat/auth errors still fail. (3) Git for Windows routes `*.docx` through the `astextplain` textconv driver, which dies on Word `~$` lock files, so `git diff --cached --quiet` returned 128 and the app never committed â€” probe now `--no-ext-diff --no-textconv`. (4) Push rejected because another machine pushed first (`persistent-memory`) â€” sign-out now retries once after `pull --rebase`, aborting and reporting if the rebase fails. Grid split into separate **Git** and **Data** status columns with per-side tooltips; `GitWorkflowResult` â†’ `WorkflowResult`. Reviewed by Kimi K3 + Grok 4.6 (both flagged "stat failure treated as skip", fixed). The app runs from `bin\Debug\net8.0-windows\InOutButton.exe` (Startup Run key) â€” `dotnet build` is the deploy step.
# Task and result contract

Use the fields that materially affect the assignment; this is a compact brief,
not a form that every small action must fill out.

The task states:
- Objective and deliverable.
- Exact input artifacts, repository root, and commit or snapshot when relevant.
- Allowed paths/actions and ownership of files if several workers can write.
- Acceptance criteria and the observations that would establish them.
- Context supplied or withheld for independent review.
- A bounded effort or deadline when the interface supports it, plus a concise
  report of unavailable prerequisites rather than an invented workaround.

The result states:
- Complete, partial, or blocked, with the deliverable or relevant artifact links.
- Findings with exact source locations, inputs, and commands/results when run.
- Changed files, failed checks, untested claims, and remaining uncertainty.
- Usage and provenance actually exposed by the tool; unknown values stay unknown.

The brief cannot expand the parent task's authorization. The parent verifies
load-bearing claims against raw artifacts and relevant independent observations.
Model diversity and agreement are supporting evidence, not proof of independence.
