# Adversarial review is assigned by stage, not by model preference

## Newest: the standing roster (Caleb, 2026-10-07)

Caleb's ruling while rewriting the session opening prompt: plans and specs
go to **Astra and/or Kimi K3**; code goes to **Grok 4.7 and/or Sol**. One
reviewer per stage is the default. Both of a pair run only when the work is
critical. The effort rule in the next section still decides how hard each
reviewer runs.

**Why:** the opening prompt had carried its own roster (Astra and Kimi on
plans, Sol on code) while this file carried the 2026-09-28 task roster
(Gemini 3.1 Pro and Kimi on plans, Grok 4.7 and Sol on code), and sessions
read both. One source of truth, here; the prompt now points at this file.

**How to apply:**

- Plans: `agent-cooperation/plan-review/plan-review.ps1 -Spec plan.md` now
  defaults to Astra alone; add `-Models astra,kimi` for critical work.
- Code: one Grok 4.7 pass in consult mode, or one Sol review through Codex,
  at the effort the next section sets; both for critical work, then one
  integration review of the merged batch.
- Gemini 3.1 Pro, and Grok on plans, leave the standing roster. Either is
  available when Caleb asks for it on a task.
- This settles the 2026-09-28 librarian CHECK (Astra against Gemini plus
  Kimi on plans) and the 2026-09-18 CHECK about the `efficient-fable`
  skill's council description, which now matches this file.

## Effort scales with the job (Caleb, 2026-10-02)

The rule, in Caleb's words: "A high-effort pass is only needed for
something both truly critical and large - otherwise we should be scaling
our compute to the importance of the task." Both conditions, not either:
critical-but-small work (this one) and large-but-routine work get low or
medium effort. This applies to every reviewer and every stage, and it
overrides the blanket "run at high effort" in the 2026-09-28 section below.

On a small, high-stakes job (renaming sample IDs in nine lab instrument
files, `scratch_pad/misc/block2_renumber_100226`), Fable launched two
parallel Grok 4.7 code reviews at high effort, 10-15 minutes each, one on
the tool and one on the verifier and its tests. Caleb: "I think we're
burning way too much compute doing these 10-15 minute adversarial reviews
on Grok." Both were stopped a few minutes in and replaced by one low-effort
pass on the tool.

**Why:** the 2026-09-28 "high effort, two rounds" ruling was made on large
multi-file deliverables with shipped money numbers. Carried over to a
500-line single-purpose script, it bought little: the measured routing data
already shows Grok 4.7 at low finding about as many real issues as at high
(3.9 against 3.8 per review, half the claims;
[[grok-consult-prompt-size-and-effort]]).

**How to apply:**
- Size the review to the work. For a small or single-file job, one Grok
  pass at low on the code that runs; high only when the work is both truly
  critical and large.
- Don't send tests to Grok when mutation tests and failure-path runs already
  measure them; the measurement is the stronger evidence.
- A second round runs only when round one led to changes, and then at low
  or medium on the diff.
- Plan reviews stayed cheap and useful here (Gemini 3.1 Pro and Kimi K3 at
  high, about 3 minutes each on an 8k-character spec); the cost problem is
  Grok's long high-effort reasoning, not the council.

