# Blunt and technically correct, at every level

Caleb, **2026-09-17**, reviewing the project-atlas proposals:

> "I'd like blunt but technically correct descriptions (detailed where the
> explanation requires detail) at every level of this project, and in
> general across all projects."

**Why:** status text drifts two ways. One is flattery: "works" for a thing
nobody has run since the last refactor. The other is vagueness dressed as
brevity: a short line that hides the mechanism. He wants neither. A short
surface (a tooltip, an index line) must still be exact, and a body must go
as deep as the mechanism needs.

**How to apply:**

- Say what is true, how we know, and if it doesn't work, why. "Doesn't
  work yet because the schema loader hasn't been built." "Worked in v1.
  Not updated or tested for v2."
- Match length to the surface, not to a style rule. Tooltip: two
  sentences. Unit body: as long as the explanation needs.
- Separate what a thing does from how we know it. "Works, claimed" and
  "works, verified 2026-09-10" are different statements.
- Ban frills, hedging, jargon, and project-only shorthand. Write for
  someone who has never opened the repo. Never cut detail to hit a length.
- Related: [[ai-involved-not-ai-generated]], [[explain-outside-core-domains]],
  [[frame-current-vs-future-constraints]].
